{
  "requirement": "Validation MUST report a requirement or example with a missing closing fence or an empty body. Full names of `{req}` and `{example}` definitions MUST be unique across requirements, examples, and named definitions. Habeas MUST report every duplicate full name, including collisions involving named definitions, and identify both the duplicate declaration and the first declaration.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Implementation: complete, but the testing has a gap, so the judgment is false.**\n\n**What the implementation does**\n- `collect_definitions` (src/evidence.rs:350-495) is marked `{impl}` and carries out every obligation.\n- It reports a missing closing fence: `block.closed` is false → \"`<kind> <name> is missing a closing colon fence`\". This applies to requirements, examples and definitions.\n- It reports an empty body: the trimmed text is empty → \"`<kind> <name> has no text`\".\n- It reports every duplicate full name. All kinds share one `BTreeMap`, including `DefinitionKind::Term`, so collisions with named definitions are caught. Each duplicate is reported with \"`duplicate <kind> <name>; first declared at <source>`\", which identifies both the duplicate declaration and the first.\n- `load_definitions` (src/evidence.rs:139-155) aggregates these errors. It is marked `{impl}`.\n- The other behavior in this function is covered by other `{impl}` marks on the same function. These are invalid names (`ci.identifiers`), unsupported options (`references.anchors`), term syntax (`definitions.declaration`), and example citations (`examples.definition`).\n\n**Tests that are marked**\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` covers a duplicate requirement, with the first declaration named at `md/requirements.md:3`, and an empty requirement body.\n- `reports_a_missing_closing_colon_fence` covers an unclosed requirement.\n- `example_errors_include_duplicates_invalid_bodies_and_missing_tests` covers requirement/example collisions, an example/example duplicate with its first declaration, an empty example, and an unclosed example.\n- `discovery_errors_fail_before_configuration_or_inference` (tests/authoring_batch.rs) covers an unclosed requirement through the CLI.\n\n**Gap**\n- The requirement's distinguishing obligation is that collisions involving **named definitions** are reported, with both declarations identified.\n- None of the four `{test}`-marked tests exercises a `{definition}` collision.\n- The only such test is `invalid_declarations_and_reference_targets_are_reported` in tests/definitions.rs. It declares a `{req} x` and a `{definition} x` in one chapter. It is marked only for `requirements.definitions.declaration` and `definitions.references`, not for this requirement.\n- It also asserts only the substring \"duplicate definition bad.x\". It does not check the \"first declared at …\" part for a term collision, or a collision where the first declaration is a term.\n- Missing-fence and empty-body behavior for `{definition}` blocks is likewise not verified under this requirement. The unclosed-definition test is marked only for `definitions.declaration`.\n\n**What would close it**\n- Mark a named-definition collision test with `{test}` for this requirement.\n- Make it assert both the duplicate and the first-declaration location, in both orders (term first, and requirement or example first).",
  "inputs": [
